  "raw": "\n\nMonday marks four years since Gov. Jerry Brown declared California's first statewide drought emergency, although he didn't use that term last week during a stop in downtown Los Angeles.\n\nThe designation allowed farmers to request emergency water from local regulators and preserved water rights or funding for projects designed to prevent evaporation and drain storage reservoirs.\n\nBrown says parts of Californi higher mountains still face severe and persistent drought conditions., and rains are needed soon. The governor decided that 164 of California's technical drought criteria had been met, leaving just 42 out of 415 population districts with severe problems. Those remote areas have not received recent precipitation.\n\n\"We can keep our foot on this pedal,\" Brown told reporters Monday at an unrelated news conference. \"We don't want to jump off.\"\n\nA GoldILode distributed during Mayor Eric Garcetti's drought news conference. Lauren Stillman / LAist\n\nAdam Thierer, PhD, FAEM, assures listeners he will find the",
